{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785911307102", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-9c3aa54a32789b73", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "curl -sL \"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.03264v1\" 2>&1 | python3 -c \"\nimport sys, re\nhtml = sys.stdin.read()\n# Remove HTML tags but keep text\ntext = re.sub(r'<script[^>]*>.*?</script>', '', html, flags=re.DOTALL)\ntext = re.sub(r'<style[^>]*>.*?</style>', '', text, flags=re.DOTALL)\ntext = re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', ' ', text)\ntext = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', text)\n# Print from 'Method' section onward\nidx = text.find('3. Method')\nif idx == -1:\n    idx = text.find('Preliminaries')\nif idx >= 0:\n    print(text[idx:idx+15000])\nelse:\n    print('Method section not found')\n    print(text[:5000])\n\" 2>&1", "description": "Extract method and experiment text from HTML", "timeout": 30000}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fd00f07a-a951-4a14-b785-b88da338a3ae", "uuid": "aeeefcb2-3cfb-48d7-9072-44230a9646f1"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9c3aa54a32789b73", "type": "tool_result", "content": "3. Method 3.1. Preliminaries State Space Models (SSMs), such as S4 (Gu et al. , 2021 ) and Mamba (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) , are structured sequence models that integrate the core principles of recurrent and convolutional architectures. They achieve linear or near-linear computational complexity with respect to sequence length, making them highly efficient for long-range sequence modeling. These models originate from continuous-time dynamical systems and define a mathematical mapping from an input sequence u ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ L u(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} to an output sequence y ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ L y(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} through a hidden state h ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ N h(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N} , where t t denotes time. Formally, an SSM can be expressed as a continuous-time Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE) (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) : (1) h ′ ​ ( t ) \\displaystyle h^{\\prime}(t) = 𝐀 ​ h ​ ( t ) + 𝐁 ​ u ​ ( t ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{A}h(t)+\\mathbf{B}u(t), y ​ ( t ) \\displaystyle y(t) = 𝐂 ​ h ​ ( t ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{C}h(t), where h ​ ( t ) h(t) is the hidden state, h ′ ​ ( t ) h^{\\prime}(t) is its temporal derivative, and u ​ ( t ) u(t) and y ​ ( t ) y(t) denote the input and output, respectively. The matrices 𝐀 ∈ ℝ N × N \\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times N} , 𝐁 ∈ ℝ N × 1 \\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times 1} , and 𝐂 ∈ ℝ 1 × N \\mathbf{C}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times N} represent the state transition, input projection, and output projection. To effectively apply SSMs in discrete-time learning frameworks, the continuous system must first be discretized into a sequence-to-sequence formulation. Both S4 (Gu et al. , 2021 ) and Mamba (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) employ a Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) discretization scheme, introducing a timescale parameter Δ \\Delta that controls the state transition speed and determines how quickly information propagates through time, Intuitively, a larger Δ \\Delta forces the model to focus on the current input to capture abrupt state transitions, while a smaller Δ \\Delta preserves historical context during stable periods. . The continuous parameters 𝐀 \\mathbf{A} and 𝐁 \\mathbf{B} are subsequently discretized into 𝐀 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{A}} and 𝐁 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{B}} as follows: (2) 𝐀 ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\mathbf{A}} = exp ⁡ ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) , \\displaystyle=\\exp(\\Delta\\mathbf{A}), 𝐁 ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\mathbf{B}} = ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) − 1 ​ ( exp ⁡ ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) − 𝐈 ) ​ Δ ​ 𝐁 , \\displaystyle=(\\Delta\\mathbf{A})^{-1}\\big(\\exp(\\Delta\\mathbf{A})-\\mathbf{I}\\big)\\,\\Delta\\mathbf{B}, h t \\displaystyle h_{t} = 𝐀 ¯ ​ h t − 1 + 𝐁 ¯ ​ u t , \\displaystyle=\\bar{\\mathbf{A}}h_{t-1}+\\bar{\\mathbf{B}}u_{t}, y t \\displaystyle y_{t} = 𝐂 ​ h t . \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{C}h_{t}. Although both S4 and Mamba follow the same principle, Mamba introduces input-dependent parameterization through the Selective Scan (S6) mechanism. Specifically, the parameters Δ ∈ ℝ b × L × D \\Delta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times D} , 𝐁 ∈ ℝ b × L × N \\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times N} , and 𝐂 ∈ ℝ b × L × N \\mathbf{C}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times N} are conditioned on the input sequence u t ∈ ℝ b × L × D u_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times D} , where b b denotes the batch size, L L is the sequence length, and D D represents the feature dimension. 3.2. Overall Architectures Given a video sequence, we partition it into non-overlapping video clips { X t } t = 1 T \\{X_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and their corresponding audio streams { A t } t = 1 T \\{A_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} , where T T denotes the sequence length. As illustrated in Figure 2 , following the prior workes (Cheng et al. , 2022 ; Heo et al. , 2022 ) , our method takes { X t } t = 1 T \\{X_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and { A t } t = 1 T \\{A_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} as inputs and aims to segment instance masks of sounding objects { M t , i } t = 1 , i = 1 T , N t \\{M_{t,i}\\}_{t=1,i=1}^{T,N_{t}} , where N t N_{t} represents the number of instances at frame t t . The video signal is first encoded by a pretrained backbone network, yielding multi-scale video features { F v i } i = 1 4 \\{F_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} . Here, F v i ∈ ℝ B × T × D × H i × W i F_{v}^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times T\\times D\\times H_{i}\\times W_{i}} , where B B denotes the batch size, D D is the feature dimension, and H i , W i H_{i},W_{i} represent the spatial dimensions at stage i i . To preserve fine-grained details crucial for instance discrimination, the audio signal is fed into the ASD (as described in Section 3.3.1 ) for sound source separation and feature extraction, producing audio features { F a i } i = 1 N a \\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} . Here, F a i ∈ ℝ B × T × D F_{a}^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times T\\times D} , where N a N_{a} represents the number of separated sound sources. These two features are then passed into T T frame-level sound source localizers to localize sounding objects. The video features are input to a pixel decoder for multi-scale interaction, yielding refined features { F ~ v i } i = 1 4 \\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} . Subsequently, these refined features and audio features are jointly fed into the HCM (as described in Section 3.3.2 ) for cross-model correspondence and fusion. Frame-level object queries are then extracted using a transformer decoder. Finally, { F ~ v i } i = 1 4 \\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} , queries from all frames, and { F a i } i = 1 N a \\left\\{F_{a}^{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} are sent to the video-level sounding instance tracker for matching and tracking sounding instances. After passing through the video encoder, the queries and { F a i } i = 1 N a \\left\\{F_{a}^{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} are input to the ADM (as described in Section 3.4 ), which incorporates dynamic variations from both video and audio modalities to enhance the model’s reasoning capability. A transformer decoder then extracts video-level queries to predict classes, which are combined with the { F ~ v i } i = 1 4 \\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} to predict masks. 3.3. Acoustic-Semantic Projector To enable the model to identify individual instances clearly while ensuring accurate cross-model matching, we propose the ASP. This model comprises two components: the Audio Source Disentanglement (ASD) and the Hierarchical Correspondence Mechanism (HCM). The ASD focuses on separating audio sources, while the HCM establishes multi-model relationships between audio and video. Note that the ASD is executed once, whereas the HCM operates multiple times within the frame-level sound source localization. 3.3.1. Audio Source Disentanglement Audio perception confronts a fundamental challenge. When multiple sounding instances vocalize simultaneously, their sounds convolve with each other or with unknown acoustic reverberation functions due to the physical nature of sound. They mix in acoustic sensors in an inseparable manner, forming a single entangled audio signal. Such audio makes it difficult to distinguish different instances when establishing cross-modal correspondences with video, and this one-to-many relationship is inherently problematic. Some methods attempt to split a single audio feature into multiple query representations using cross-attention, but this approach lacks explicit sound source supervision and relies solely on the network’s self-learning. In contrast, our method employs MixIT (Wisdom et al. , 2020 ) , a sound source separation model pretrained on YFCC100M (Wisdom et al. , 2020 ) , to separate audio sources. This approach explicitly disentangles individual sound sources. The separated sources are then converted into mel-spectrograms and fed into a pretrained VGGish (Hershey et al. , 2017 ) model for feature extraction, yielding audio features { F a i } i = 1 N a \\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} . Note that both the source separation model and feature extraction model remain frozen during this process, which is executed only once. Figure 3 . Structure of the Hierarchical Correspondence Mechanism (HCM). For clarity, the figure shows only three clusters each for audio and video, and three audio feature streams. In practice, the number of clusters and streams exceeds three. The figure illustrates the structure of the Hierarchical Correspondence Mechanism (HCM). Figure 4 . Structure of the Asynchronous Dynamics Modulator (ADM). (a) Overall structure of ADM. (b) Structure of Audio-Dynamically Modulated Mamba (AMM). (c) Data flow in Audio-Dynamically Modulated SSM (AM-SSM). The figure illustrates the structure of the Asynchronous Dynamics Modulator (ADM). 3.3.2. Hierarchical Correspondence Mechanism The heterogeneity between audio and video modalities results in distinct feature characteristics: video features prioritize spatial representation, while audio features emphasize temporal summarization. However, both modalities share a common semantic ground, as their semantic information jointly refers to the scene constructed in the video. To address this, we propose the HCM, which first establishes connections at the semantic semantic level, then maps these connections back to the spatial domain to obtain precise spatial correspondences, and performs multi-model interaction. The detailed structure is illustrated in the Figure 3 . Given the refined video features { F ~ v i } i = 1 4 \\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} and audio features { F a i } i = 1 N a \\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} , we first extract features from the top three stages. We then apply k-means clustering to both video and audio features separately, mapping them into a shared semantic space to obtain audio cluster { A ​ C i } i = 1 C k ​ a \\{AC_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{C_{ka}} and visual cluster { V ​ C i } i = 1 C k ​ v \\{VC_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{C_{kv}} , where C k ​ a C_{ka} and C k ​ v C_{kv} represent the number of audio and visual clusters. Next, we compute the correlation between cluster centroids and generate approximate importance scores P P weighted by the number of tokens within each cluster, as shown below: (3) { A ​ C i } i = 1 C k ​ a , I a \\displaystyle\\{AC_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{C_{ka}},I_{a} = Kmeans ​ ( { F a i } i = 1 N a , C k ​ a ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Kmeans}(\\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}},C_{ka}), { V ​ C i } i = 1 C k ​ v , I v \\displaystyle\\{VC_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{C_{kv}},I_{v} = Kmeans ​ ( { F ~ v i } i = 2 4 , C k ​ v ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Kmeans}(\\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=2}^{4},C_{kv}), C i ​ j \\displaystyle C_{ij} = centroid ​ ( A ​ C i ) × centroid ​ ( V ​ C j ) T D , \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\mathrm{centroid}(AC_{i})\\times\\mathrm{centroid}(VC_{j})^{T}}{\\sqrt{D}}, P \\displaystyle P = V ​ C j ​ exp ⁡ ( C i ​ j ) ∑ k = 1 C k ​ v V ​ C k ​ exp ⁡ ( C i ​ k ) , \\displaystyle=\\frac{VC_{j}\\exp(C_{ij})}{{\\textstyle\\sum_{k=1}^{C_{kv}}}VC_{k}\\exp(C_{ik})}, where I a I_{a} and I v I_{v} represent the mapping from original tokens to their corresponding clusters. Considering that both audio and video contain redundant background information that interferes with foreground learning during segmentation, we employ the Top-p algorithm to filter importance scores and retain crucial audio-visual semantic associations. By combining the previously obtained cluster to token mappings I a I_{a} and I v I_{v} , we project the semantic-level associations back to the spatial domain, yielding a correlation index matrix. Finally, we use this matrix with sparse matrix multiplication to compute attention between audio and video, achieving more accurate audio-visual fusion as follows: (4) I ​ n ​ d ​ e ​ x \\displaystyle Index = SP ​ ( TopP ​ ( P , p ) , I a , I v ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{SP}(\\mathrm{TopP}(P,p),I_{a},I_{v}), A ​ M \\displaystyle AM = SMM ​ ( { F a i } i = 1 N a , { F ~ v i } i = 2 4 T , I ​ n ​ d ​ e ​ x ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{SMM}(\\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}},{\\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=2}^{4}}^{T},Index), o ​ u ​ t \\displaystyle out = SMM ​ ( Softmax ​ ( A ​ M ) , { F ~ v i } i = 2 4 , Index ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{SMM}(\\mathrm{Softmax}(AM),\\{\\tilde{F}_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=2}^{4},\\mathrm{Index}), where SP ​ ( u , v , w ) \\mathrm{SP}(u,v,w) denotes the spatial mapping of u u based on the relationship between v v and w w , TopP ​ ( u , p ) \\mathrm{TopP}(u,p) represents filtering u u using the Top-P algorithm with probability p p , and SMM ​ ( u , v , Index ) \\mathrm{SMM}(u,v,\\mathrm{Index}) indicates computing the sparse matrix multiplication between u u and v v only at valid positions specified by Index \\mathrm{Index} . 3.4. Asynchronous Dynamics Modulator To model the asynchronous and complex dynamic characteristics of audio and video, we propose a Mamba-based Asynchronous Dynamics Modulator (ADM). We examine Mamba’s internal parameters and find that Δ \\Delta directly controls the state transition speed in Mamba, determining how quickly information propagates through time. This property allows Δ \\Delta to flexibly adapt to different temporal scales and capture the asynchronous nature of audio-visual dynamics. Our approach leverages Mamba’s characteristics by first modeling video dynamics, then extracting audio dynamics and using them to control Mamba’s Δ \\Delta parameter, thereby achieving synchronized perception of audio-visual dynamics. The structure of ADM is illustrated in Figure 4 (a), consisting of our proposed Audio-Dynamically Modulated Mamba (AMM), feed-forward network (FFN), layer normalization, and residual connections. Our AMM follows the common Mamba block design from prior workes, as shown in Figure 4 (b). Our core improvement lies in the Audio-Dynamically Modulated SSM (AM-SSM), as shown in Figure 4 (c), which enables modeling of asynchronous audio-visual dynamics. After object queries { Q t } t = 1 T \\{Q_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} are input into the AM-SSM, where Q t ∈ ℝ B × N q × D Q_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times N_{q}\\times D} and N q N_{q} denotes the query number, they undergo dimension transformation through a linear layer, which then splits into content-aware 𝐁 \\mathbf{B} , 𝐂 \\mathbf{C} , and Δ \\Delta parameters. The Δ \\Delta at this stage captures the internal dynamics of the video. For audio features { F a i } i = 1 N a \\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}} , we first apply a linear layer to transform their dimensions to match those of the video features. Note that the video has already undergone cross-scan for four-directional flattening. To maintain consistency, audio features also require flattening. We then employ grouped convolution and MLP to extract dynamic variations in the audio, represented as gating values. After sigmoid activation, these values are multiplied with the audio features and added to the original Δ \\Delta . This enables the updated Δ ¯ \\bar{\\Delta} to simultaneously perceive both video and audio dynamics. Subsequently, we use Δ ¯ \\bar{\\Delta} to perform the SSM modeling process, as follows: (5) 𝐁 , 𝐂 , Δ \\displaystyle\\mathbf{B},\\mathbf{C},\\Delta = Split ​ ( Linear ​ ( { Q t } t = 1 T ) ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Split}(\\mathrm{Linear}(\\{Q_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T})), F ~ a \\displaystyle\\tilde{F}_{a} = CS ​ ( Linear ​ ( { F a i } i = 1 N a ) ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{CS}(\\mathrm{Linear}(\\{F_{a}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}})), Δ ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\Delta} = Δ + F ~ a × Sigmoid ​ ( M", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fd00f07a-a951-4a14-b785-b88da338a3ae", "uuid": "7aa46148-f180-42cc-b293-a670ab4a33e1", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T06:28:33.482Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "3. Method 3.1. Preliminaries State Space Models (SSMs), such as S4 (Gu et al. , 2021 ) and Mamba (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) , are structured sequence models that integrate the core principles of recurrent and convolutional architectures. They achieve linear or near-linear computational complexity with respect to sequence length, making them highly efficient for long-range sequence modeling. These models originate from continuous-time dynamical systems and define a mathematical mapping from an input sequence u ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ L u(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} to an output sequence y ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ L y(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L} through a hidden state h ​ ( t ) ∈ ℝ N h(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N} , where t t denotes time. Formally, an SSM can be expressed as a continuous-time Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE) (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) : (1) h ′ ​ ( t ) \\displaystyle h^{\\prime}(t) = 𝐀 ​ h ​ ( t ) + 𝐁 ​ u ​ ( t ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{A}h(t)+\\mathbf{B}u(t), y ​ ( t ) \\displaystyle y(t) = 𝐂 ​ h ​ ( t ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{C}h(t), where h ​ ( t ) h(t) is the hidden state, h ′ ​ ( t ) h^{\\prime}(t) is its temporal derivative, and u ​ ( t ) u(t) and y ​ ( t ) y(t) denote the input and output, respectively. The matrices 𝐀 ∈ ℝ N × N \\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times N} , 𝐁 ∈ ℝ N × 1 \\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times 1} , and 𝐂 ∈ ℝ 1 × N \\mathbf{C}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times N} represent the state transition, input projection, and output projection. To effectively apply SSMs in discrete-time learning frameworks, the continuous system must first be discretized into a sequence-to-sequence formulation. Both S4 (Gu et al. , 2021 ) and Mamba (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) employ a Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) (Gu and Dao, 2023 ) discretization scheme, introducing a timescale parameter Δ \\Delta that controls the state transition speed and determines how quickly information propagates through time, Intuitively, a larger Δ \\Delta forces the model to focus on the current input to capture abrupt state transitions, while a smaller Δ \\Delta preserves historical context during stable periods. . The continuous parameters 𝐀 \\mathbf{A} and 𝐁 \\mathbf{B} are subsequently discretized into 𝐀 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{A}} and 𝐁 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{B}} as follows: (2) 𝐀 ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\mathbf{A}} = exp ⁡ ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) , \\displaystyle=\\exp(\\Delta\\mathbf{A}), 𝐁 ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\mathbf{B}} = ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) − 1 ​ ( exp ⁡ ( Δ ​ 𝐀 ) − 𝐈 ) ​ Δ ​ 𝐁 , \\displaystyle=(\\Delta\\mathbf{A})^{-1}\\big(\\exp(\\Delta\\mathbf{A})-\\mathbf{I}\\big)\\,\\Delta\\mathbf{B}, h t \\displaystyle h_{t} = 𝐀 ¯ ​ h t − 1 + 𝐁 ¯ ​ u t , \\displaystyle=\\bar{\\mathbf{A}}h_{t-1}+\\bar{\\mathbf{B}}u_{t}, y t \\displaystyle y_{t} = 𝐂 ​ h t . \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{C}h_{t}. Although both S4 and Mamba follow the same principle, Mamba introduces input-dependent parameterization through the Selective Scan (S6) mechanism. Specifically, the parameters Δ ∈ ℝ b × L × D \\Delta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times D} , 𝐁 ∈ ℝ b × L × N \\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times N} , and 𝐂 ∈ ℝ b × L × N \\mathbf{C}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times N} are conditioned on the input sequence u t ∈ ℝ b × L × D u_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times L\\times D} , where b b denotes the batch size, L L is the sequence length, and D D represents the feature dimension. 3.2. Overall Architectures Given a video sequence, we partition it into non-overlapping video clips { X t } t = 1 T \\{X_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and their corresponding audio streams { A t } t = 1 T \\{A_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} , where T T denotes the sequence length. As illustrated in Figure 2 , following the prior workes (Cheng et al. , 2022 ; Heo et al. , 2022 ) , our method takes { X t } t = 1 T \\{X_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and { A t } t = 1 T \\{A_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} as inputs and aims to segment instance masks of sounding objects { M t , i } t = 1 , i = 1 T , N t \\{M_{t,i}\\}_{t=1,i=1}^{T,N_{t}} , where N t N_{t} represents the number of instances at frame t t . The video signal is first encoded by a pretrained backbone network, yielding multi-scale video features { F v i } i = 1 4 \\{F_{v}^{i}\\}_{i=1}^{4} . Here, F v i ∈ ℝ B × T × D × H i × W i F_{v}^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times T\\times D\\times H_{i}\\times W_{i}} , where B B denotes the batch size, D D is the feature dimension, and H i , W i H_{i},W_{i} represent the spatial dimensions at stage i i . To preserve fine-grained details crucia