da_{\\text{video}} , and λ sim \\lambda_{\\text{sim}} are set to 1, 1, and 0.5, respectively. For frame-level supervision, we compute matching costs between frame queries and ground-truth masks using the Mask2Former (Cheng et al. , 2022 ) formulation, followed by Hungarian matching (Kuhn, 1955 ) as in DETR (Carion et al. , 2020 ) . For video-level supervision, we match video queries with object trajectories following IFC (Hwang et al. , 2021 ) to enforce temporal consistency. Additionally, a similarity loss (Yang et al. , 2019 ; Heo et al. , 2022 ) aligns frame and video queries in the embedding space, encouraging features of the same identity to be close while separating those of different identities. 4. Experiments 4.1. Datasets &amp; Evaluation Metrics Datasets. We evaluate our proposed method on the AVISeg dataset (Guo et al. , 2025 ) , a large-scale benchmark for audio-visual instance segmentation. AVISeg contains 926 videos with diverse and challenging scenarios, covering 26 sound categories across “Music”, “Speaking”, “Machine”, and “Animal”. Each video is annotated with pixel-level sounding object masks using a semi-automatic SAM-based tool (Kirillov et al. , 2023 ) and refined manually for quality assurance. The dataset includes long videos (average 61.4s) and is officially split into 616, 105, and 205 videos for training, validation, and testing. Evaluation Metrics. We evaluate model performance using mean Average Precision (mAP) (Yang et al. , 2019 ) , Higher Order Tracking Accuracy (HOTA) (Luiten et al. , 2021 ) , and Frame-level Sound Localization Accuracy (FSLA) (Guo et al. , 2025 ) . mAP measures detection quality across trajectories, while HOTA jointly evaluates detection and association consistency. FSLA assesses frame-wise localization accuracy by checking whether the predicted categories, object counts, and IoU exceed a given threshold α \\alpha , and includes variants (FSLAn, FSLAs, FSLAm) for silent, single-source, and multi-source scenarios. 4.2. Implementation Details Following prior work, we validate the effectiveness of H2S using ResNet-50 (He et al. , 2016 ) and Swin-L (Liu et al. , 2021 ) as visual backbones pretrained on ImageNet-1K (Deng et al. , 2009 ) and MS-COCO (Lin et al. , 2014 ) , and VGGish (Hershey et al. , 2017 ) as the audio backbone pretrained on AudioSet. In ASD, we set N a N_{a} to 8. In HCM, we set C k ​ v C_{kv} and C k ​ a C_{ka} to 8, and configure p p as 0.7, 0.8, and 0.9 from top to bottom stages. We train our model on the AVISeg for 48,000 iterations using the AdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017 ) optimizer with a base learning rate of 0.0001. The learning rate is reduced by a factor of 10 at iteration 32,000. The batch size is set to 2, with each training sample containing 5 frames. For data augmentation, we apply multi-scale augmentation and random horizontal flipping. During training, the shorter side of input frames is randomly scaled between 360 and 480 pixels while maintaining the aspect ratio. During testing, the shorter side is fixed at 360 pixels. All experiments are conducted on two RTX 6000 GPUs. Table 2 . Quantitative comparison with AVISM (Guo et al. , 2025 ) under different backbones and pretraining datasets. Here, Ba. denotes the visual backbone, and Pr. denotes the pretraining dataset. Method Ba. Pr. Param. GFLOPs FPS FSLA HOTA mAP AVISM (G\n=== LOSS FUNCTION ===\n3.5. Loss Function Following previous works (Guo et al. , 2025 ) , we adopt a joint loss composed of frame-level, video-level, and similarity terms: (6) ℒ = λ frame ​ ℒ frame + λ video ​ ℒ video + λ sim ​ ℒ sim , \\mathcal{L}=\\lambda_{\\text{frame}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{frame}}+\\lambda_{\\text{video}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{video}}+\\lambda_{\\text{sim}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{sim}}, where λ frame \\lambda_{\\text{frame}} , λ video \\lambda_{\\text{video}} , and λ sim \\lambda_{\\text{sim}} are set to 1, 1, and 0.5, respectively. For frame-level supervision, we compute matching costs between frame queries an