king Accuracy (HOTA) (Luiten et al. , 2021 ) , and Frame-level Sound Localization Accuracy (FSLA) (Guo et al. , 2025 ) . mAP measures detection quality across trajectories, while HOTA jointly evaluates detection and association consistency. FSLA assesses frame-wise localization accuracy by checking whether the predicted categories, object counts, and IoU exceed a given threshold α \\alpha , and includes variants (FSLAn, FSLAs, FSLAm) for silent, single-source, and multi-source scenarios. 4.2. Implementation Details Following prior work, we validate the effectiveness of H2S using ResNet-50 (He et al. , 2016 ) and Swin-L (Liu et al. , 2021 ) as visual backbones pretrained on ImageNet-1K (Deng et al. , 2009 ) and MS-COCO (Lin et al. , 2014 ) , and VGGish (Hershey et al. , 2017 ) as the audio backbone pretrained on AudioSet. In ASD, we set N a N_{a} to 8. In HCM, we set C k ​ v C_{kv} and C k ​ a C_{ka} to 8, and configure p p as 0.7, 0.8, and 0.9 from top to bottom stages. We train our model on the AVISeg for 48,000 iterations using the AdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017 ) optimizer with a base learning rate of 0.0001. The learning rate is reduced by a factor of 10 at iteration 32,000. The batch size is set to 2, with each training sample containing 5 frames. For data augmentation, we apply multi-scale augmentation and random horizontal flipping. During training, the shorter side of input frames is randomly scaled between 360 and 480 pixels while maintaining the aspect ratio. During testing, the shorter side is fixed at 360 pixels. All experiments are conducted on two RTX 6000 GPUs. Table 2 . Quantitative comparison with AVISM (Guo et al. , 2025 ) under different backbones and pretraining datasets. Here, Ba. denotes the visual backbone, and Pr. denotes the pretraining dataset. Method Ba. Pr. Param. GFLOPs FPS FSLA HOTA mAP AVISM (G\n=== LOSS FUNCTION ===\n3.5. Loss Function Following previous works (Guo et al. , 2025 ) , we adopt a joint loss composed of frame-level, v